Ideally, given a lens and a reference to the context, we'd like to be able to get a reference to the lensed data. This would be trivial if all lensed data was owned by the context, but unfortunately (and very necessarily) we have the `Map` lens, which lets us derive data that isn't stored in the context. This means that it's not possible to define a lifetime for a reference which could be associated either with the lifetime of the context, or with the lifetime of the map lens.

To solve this issue this PR introduces a new enum type called `LensValue`. Similar to `Cow`, this type can be either a borrowed reference (with the lifetime of the context), or an owned value. Most lenses will return a `LensValue::Borrowed`, as their data is retrieved from the context, while some lenses, like `Map`, will return a `LensValue::Owned`. The `LensValue` implements `Deref`, which allows us to get a reference to the inner data.

~~To complicate things a little, we have the `Res` trait which is used when we want to pass either a value or a lens to some function (modifier or view constructor). To get a reference there are now 3 possibilities. If it's a lens then it could be borrowed or owned, and then there's the case where it's just a value. To solve this I've introduced another variant to the `LensValue`, which is `LensValue::Local`.~~

This changes to lens `view` signature to the following:

```rust
fn view<'b>(&self, source: &'a Self::Source) -> Option<LensValue<'a, Self::Target>>;
```

The `get()` method has been moved from the `Lens` trait to the `Res` trait and returns an owned `T`, copying/cloning if necessary (can panic if the data doesn't exist). A `get_ref` method has been added to `Res` which returns `Option<LensValue<T>>`, which can be used to get a reference to the lensed/owned data.

Here's an example demonstrating how a lens or value can now be stored in a view and a reference to the stored data can be used in the `draw` method:

```rust
#[derive(Lens, Model, Setter)]
pub struct AppData {
    pub some_data: String,
}

pub struct CustomView<R> {
    value_or_lens: R,
}

impl<R: 'static + Res<String>> CustomView<R> {
    pub fn new(cx: &mut Context, value_or_lens: R) -> Handle<Self> {
        value_or_lens.set_or_bind(cx, cx.current(), |cx, _, _| cx.needs_redraw());
        Self { value_or_lens }.build(cx, |_| {})
    }
}

impl<L: 'static + Res<String>> View for CustomView<L> {
    fn draw(&self, cx: &mut DrawContext, _canvas: &mut Canvas) {
        if let Some(data) = self.value_or_lens.get_ref(cx) {
            // Deref to a reference
            println!("{:?}", *data);
        }
    }
}

fn main() {
    Application::new(|cx| {
        AppData { some_data: String::from("Hello") }.build(cx);

        // Pass a lens
        CustomView::new(cx, AppData::some_data);

        // Pass a value
        CustomView::new(cx, String::from("test"));
    })
    .run();
}
```

